###  https://w3id.org/bmp#AirDryTimber
bmp:AirDryTimber rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf bmp:WoodTimber ;
                 rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/77077.html"@en ,
                              "Material Category: Wood / TimberWhat it is made of / How it's made: It is made of natural wood logs (like oak, pine, or spruce) composed of cellulose fibers and lignin. It is produced by stacking freshly cut lumber outdoors under a roof for months or years, allowing the natural wind and air circulation to evaporate the moisture level down to a stable $12\\text{--}15\\%$ without using artificial kiln heat.Description: Natural wood lumber that has been seasoned by outdoor air circulation to reduce moisture and prevent warping (AirDryTimber)." ;
                 rdfs:label "AirDryTimber"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#BasaltStone
bmp:BasaltStone rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf bmp:NaturalStone ;
                rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/77077.html"@en ,
                             """Material Category: Natural Stone (Igneous Rock)
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made of dense, dark minerals rich in iron and magnesium (like pyroxene and plagioclase). It is formed when molten volcanic lava erupts onto the Earth's surface or ocean floor and cools very rapidly, leaving no time for large crystals to grow.
Description: A heavy, dark, and exceptionally hard volcanic rock with a very fine-grained texture (BasaltStone).""" ;
                rdfs:label "BasaltStone"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#Brick
bmp:Brick rdf:type owl:Class ;
          rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/32918.html"@en ,
                       """Material Category: Brick / Ceramics
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made from natural surface clay, shale, or refined earthen minerals mixed with water. This plastic mixture is shaped into blocks and dried before being baked in a high-temperature kiln, which triggers a chemical sintering process that fuses the mineral particles into a durable, vitrified, rock-hard structure.
Description: A traditional, rectangular masonry building block manufactured from raw earthen clays or silicates and hardened by extreme heat or chemical bonding, used for building structural walls and facades (Brick).""" ;
          rdfs:label "Brick"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#BrickMasonryUnit
bmp:BrickMasonryUnit rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Brick ;
                     rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/32918.html"@en ,
                                  """Material Category: Brick / Ceramics
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made from natural surface clay, shale, or refined earthen minerals mixed with water. It is pressed or extruded into a rectangular block shape and then dried and baked in a high-temperature kiln until the minerals chemically fuse into a vitrified, rock-hard structure.
Description: A standard, kiln-fired rectangular building block used for laying structural or facing brickwork (BrickMasonryUnit).""" ;
                     rdfs:label "BrickMasonryUnit"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#BulkDensity
bmp:BulkDensity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Physical .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#CalciumSilicateUnit
bmp:CalciumSilicateUnit rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Brick ;
                        rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/32918.html"@en ,
                                     """Material Category: Brick / Masonry
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made from a precise mixture of lime (calcium oxide), quartz sand, and water. Instead of being fired in a kiln like clay bricks, these units are compressed heavily into molds and then cured inside a pressurized steam chamber (an autoclave) to trigger a chemical bonding process.
Description: A durable, highly uniform, sand-lime building block cured using high-pressure steam (CalciumSilicateUnit).""" ;
                        rdfs:label "CalciumSilicateUnit"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#Cavity
bmp:Cavity rdf:type owl:Class ;
           rdfs:subClassOf bmp:LayerSet .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#ClayUnit
bmp:ClayUnit rdf:type owl:Class ;
             rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Brick ;
             rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/32918.html"@en ,
                          "Material Category: Brick / CeramicsWhat it is made of / How it's made: It is made strictly from natural kiln-firing clays and silicates. It is molded and baked at temperatures exceeding 1000 degree C, which gives it its characteristic terracotta-red or brown color and excellent weather resistance.Description: A traditional building block or tile manufactured exclusively from fired natural clay (ClayUnit)." ;
             rdfs:label "ClayUnit"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#Concrete
bmp:Concrete rdf:type owl:Class ;
             rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/41260.html"@en ,
                          """Material Category: Concrete / Cementitious Composite
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made from a precise mixture of cement binder, water, sand, and gravel aggregates. Mixing these ingredients triggers a chemical hydration reaction, causing the cement paste to harden into an artificial stone matrix that permanently locks the loose mineral aggregates into a rigid, solid structure.
Description: A heavy, artificial stone material engineered from cement, water, and mineral aggregates, known for its exceptional compressive strength once cured (Concrete).""" ;
             rdfs:label "Concrete"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#DesorptionC
bmp:DesorptionC rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf bmp:WaterVapourSorptionIsotherm .


