###  https://w3id.org/bmp#LightWeightAggregate
bmp:LightWeightAggregate rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Concrete ;
                         rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/41260.html"@en ,
                                      """Material Category: Concrete Component
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made from porous, low-density materials like expanded clay, shale, slate, pumice, or volcanic perlite. These materials are fired in a rotary kiln, causing gases inside them to expand and leave thousands of microscopic air pockets inside each stone.
Description: Highly porous, low-density stone fragments used as concrete filler to minimize dead weight (LightWeightAggregate).""" ;
                         rdfs:label "LightWeightAggregate"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#LightWeightConcrete
bmp:LightWeightConcrete rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Concrete ;
                        rdfs:comment "https://www.iso.org/standard/41260.html"@en ,
                                     """Material Category: Concrete
What it is made of / How it's made: It is made by mixing cement, water, sand, and porous aggregates (like expanded clay or pumice). Alternatively, it can be made by injecting stable foam directly into a standard cement slurry to fill the structure with tiny bubbles.
Description: A structural or insulating concrete formulated with air-filled aggregates to drastically reduce its total weight (LightWeightConcrete).""" ;
                        rdfs:label "LightWeightConcrete"@en .


###  https://w3id.org/bmp#LiquidTransportCoef
bmp:LiquidTransportCoef rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:subClassOf bmp:Hygric .


